window.AMP_ARTICLES = {
"retained-vs-contingent-physician-search": "\n<p>When a hospital or medical group needs a physician, the search model is not a footnote \u2014 it is the trail condition. Retained and contingent firms both claim they can \u201cfill the role.\u201d Only one model is structurally built to get a prepared candidate to the summit meeting with a client who is actually ready.</p>\n<p>Adaptive Medical Partners (AMP) treats retained search as guided mountaineering: the organization waits at the summit with a clear brief; candidates are hikers on the other side of the mountain; AMP is the guide who educates, filters, and stages the meeting. Contingent search often looks cheaper on day one and more expensive in missed hires, stalled committees, and half-told stories that leak into the market.</p>\n<h2>What contingent search optimizes for</h2>\n<p>Contingent search typically pays only on placement. That can sound aligned. In practice it rewards speed-to-resume over readiness-to-decide. Multiple firms may work the same role. Candidates hear conflicting stories. Clients receive volume without a shared scorecard. Marketing is thin because nobody owns the narrative long enough to invest in it.</p>\n<p>None of that means contingent is always wrong. For commodity, high-supply roles with a clean, public package, contingent can work. The failure mode appears when the seat is strategic, confidential, or competitive \u2014 exactly when you need a guide.</p>\n<h2>What retained search should buy you</h2>\n<p>A serious retained engagement buys three things that contingent rarely funds well:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Upfront commitment</strong> \u2014 both sides invest; the search gets a real workplan, not a blast.</li>\n<li><strong>Placement accountability</strong> \u2014 process milestones, candidate quality bars, and a defined path to the summit interview.</li>\n<li><strong>Marketing that protects the brand</strong> \u2014 tease-level public stories, not dossier dumps that let the market decide without you.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>AMP\u2019s retained tease is intentional: enough signal to attract the right hikers, never enough to replace a conversation with your guide. That \u201cno leaks\u201d discipline is how confidential searches stay confidential while still moving.</p>\n<h2>Fee structure without the fog</h2>\n<p>Organizations should ask any firm to separate <em>search economics</em> from <em>search craft</em>. Upfront fees fund dedicated sourcing, specialty expertise, and outbound that is not optional. Placement fees align to outcome. Marketing support \u2014 creative, job teasers, outreach sequences \u2014 should be visible in the workplan, not implied in a slide.</p>\n<p>If a proposal cannot explain what happens in weeks 1\u20133, 4\u20136, and at the first summit meeting, you are buying hope, not a trail.</p>\n<h2>Candidate experience is a client outcome</h2>\n<p>Physicians compare notes. A chaotic contingent process teaches candidates that your organization is chaotic. A guided retained process \u2014 specialty \u2192 region \u2192 controlled job tease \u2192 Tap to Talk \u2192 The Mess Responses \u2014 teaches candidates that someone is steering. AMP\u2019s mountain metaphor is not decoration; it is operating system: educate the hiker, arrive prepared, meet the client at the summit.</p>\n<h2>How to choose without theater</h2>\n<p>Compare firms on: specialty depth, confidentiality discipline, marketing craft, time-to-slate quality (not just time-to-first-resume), and whether interest actually lands in a response system humans monitor. AMP routes physician interest into <strong>The Mess Responses</strong> and client intake into <strong>The Mess</strong> plus a <strong>BD Hub</strong> path \u2014 destinations with owners, not inboxes that vanish.</p>\n<div class=\"faq\">\n<details open><summary>Is retained always more expensive?</summary><p>Retained usually costs more upfront and less in failed cycles. Total cost of vacancy plus committee time often dwarfs fee deltas.</p></details>\n<details><summary>Can AMP help if we are unsure which model fits?</summary><p>Yes. Start at the client door, share facility type and specialty, and request a meeting. You will see a retained tease \u2014 not a contract dump \u2014 before any commitment theater.</p></details>\n<details><summary>Where should physicians go from here?</summary><p>Use the physician door to search roles like the Midwest OB/GYN tease OBG-8449, or read <a href=\"#blog/evaluate-obgyn-opportunities\" data-go=\"blog/evaluate-obgyn-opportunities\">how to evaluate OB/GYN opportunities</a>.</p></details>\n</div>\n<p><em>Next steps:</em> <button class=\"linkish\" data-go=\"client\" data-trail=\"1\">I\u2019m hiring / client</button> \u00b7 <button class=\"linkish\" data-go=\"job/obg-8449\">Sample job OBG-8449</button> \u00b7 <button class=\"linkish\" data-go=\"amp-score\">AMP Score</button></p>\n\n<h2>Marketing craft is part of the fee, not a freebie</h2>\n<p>Retained search that underinvests in marketing forces candidates to discover roles through rumor. AMP treats public job pages as trail signs: cinematic enough to stop the scroll, disciplined enough to avoid leaking the full map. That means lifestyle heroes, practice-first headlines, and controlled reveals \u2014 then a human guide. Contingent blasts often invert this: maximum detail, minimum narrative ownership, and five firms saying five different things about the same seat.</p>\n<p>When organizations evaluate proposals, ask who writes the tease, who approves it, and how quickly a role can appear in a Specialty \u2192 Region \u2192 Jobs path without exposing non-public economics. If the firm cannot show a sample like OBG-8449\u2019s Monday\u2013Friday-ends-at-4 structure, they may not have a point of view \u2014 only a database.</p>\n<h2>Operational destinations beat vanity metrics</h2>\n<p>\u201cWe sent 40 candidates\u201d is not a KPI if none were summit-ready. Ask where responses live. AMP\u2019s site experience is explicit: physician interest lands in The Mess Responses; client meetings land in The Mess client intake with a BD Hub handoff. Those chips exist so leadership can see the path is built, not promised.</p>\n<p>Finally, remember the mountain story. Candidates are hikers. Clients wait at the summit. AMP educates and stages the traverse. Retained economics fund that guide work. Contingent economics often fund a race. Choose the model that matches the difficulty of the ridge you are asking someone to climb.</p>\n",
"evaluate-obgyn-opportunities": "\n<p>OB/GYN opportunities are easy to romanticize and hard to pressure-test. Brochures lead with \u201ccollegial,\u201d \u201cbusy from day one,\u201d and a city nickname. Physicians need a different trail map: schedule reality, OR/clinic mix, call burden, income structure, and whether the organization can explain those things on a short call without dumping a 40-page packet into your inbox.</p>\n<p>AMP\u2019s approach is practice-first headlines and \u22645 bullets on public pages \u2014 then Tap to Talk with a named guide. For the sample Midwest role <strong>OBG-8449</strong>, the public tease is intentionally sharp: Monday\u2013Friday that ends at 4, from $812k \u2014 with the curve, stack, and facility specifics gated.</p>\n<h2>Start with the week, not the zip code</h2>\n<p>Ask: What does Tuesday look like? When does clinic end? Who covers labor when you are not on? How often do \u201cshort days\u201d become long days? A lifestyle hero photo is useful mood; it is not evidence. The Midwest OB/GYN tease that ends at 4 is valuable precisely because it makes a falsifiable claim you can verify on a call.</p>\n<h2>Separate income band from income story</h2>\n<p>Public bands (like from $812,000) help you decide whether to spend 90 seconds. They should not pretend to be the full stack. Commencement, CME, PTO, malpractice structure, and wRVU curves belong in a confidential conversation \u2014 not on a page that competitors and patients can scrape. That is not secrecy for its own sake; it is how retained searches protect both sides of the mountain.</p>\n<h2>A five-point evaluation checklist</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Schedule integrity</strong> \u2014 written clinic hours vs. cultural hours.</li>\n<li><strong>Call architecture</strong> \u2014 frequency, backup, midwife/APP support.</li>\n<li><strong>Surgical access</strong> \u2014 block time, robotics, referral patterns.</li>\n<li><strong>Income mechanics</strong> \u2014 guarantee length, conversion, downside protection.</li>\n<li><strong>Decision path</strong> \u2014 who interviews, how fast, what \u201cyes\u201d requires.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>How AMP keeps you from drowning in PDFs</h2>\n<p>When you tap through Specialty \u2192 Region \u2192 Jobs \u2192 View, you get a world-class tease, not a dossier. Interest routes to Amy Myers for OBG-8449 (call/text 972-301-2261, email amyers@ with CC inquire@) and lands in <strong>The Mess Responses</strong>. You are never left in a dead-end form.</p>\n<h2>Red flags that look like green flags</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>\u201cCompetitive compensation\u201d with no band and no call offer.</li>\n<li>Facility names everywhere, schedule nowhere.</li>\n<li>Pressure to decide before a guide conversation.</li>\n<li>Multiple recruiters telling different versions of the same seat.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"faq\">\n<details open><summary>Should I apply on the hospital site and through AMP?</summary><p>If AMP is representing the search, let your guide coordinate. Dual paths create crossed wires and weaken confidentiality.</p></details>\n<details><summary>Is Midwest code for \u201crural only\u201d?</summary><p>No. Midwest includes community employed models with strong lifestyle upside. Read our <a href=\"#blog/midwest-lifestyle-for-physicians\" data-go=\"blog/midwest-lifestyle-for-physicians\">Midwest lifestyle guide</a>.</p></details>\n</div>\n<p><button class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-go=\"physician\" data-trail=\"1\">I\u2019m a physician \u2014 pick a trail</button>\n<button class=\"btn btn-ghost\" data-go=\"job/obg-8449\">Open OBG-8449 tease</button></p>\n\n<h2>A note on Education and substance</h2>\n<p>Search engines reward helpful structure: clear H1/H2s, questions people actually ask, and helpful links to real next steps. This Education hub is packed on purpose \u2014 not as thin filler, but as trail markers toward the physician door, the client door, and sample opportunities like OBG-8449. Soft CTAs beat hard dossier dumps. When you are ready, Adaptive Medical Partners meets you with a guide, not a brochure cannon.</p>\n<p>Physicians comparing regions should also skim compensation clarity, schedule integrity, and summit readiness \u2014 the same themes inside AMP Score. Organizations should pair this reading with a retained process conversation so marketing, sourcing, and leadership share one brief. The mountain metaphor stays consistent: hikers on one side, clients at the summit, AMP guiding the traverse so the meeting is earned and prepared.</p>\n<p>If you take nothing else from this article, take the destination discipline. Every serious clickthrough on this site ends in The Mess and/or BD Hub. That is how a world-class recruiting site behaves when it is built for operations, not just aesthetics.</p>\n\n<h2>Translate brochure language into clinical reality</h2>\n<p>\u201cBusy from day one\u201d can mean healthy referrals \u2014 or an understaffed call pool. \u201cSupportive administration\u201d can mean responsive leadership \u2014 or silence until budget season. Write down the claim, then ask your AMP guide what evidence would confirm it. On a world-class tease page you should see at most five bullets that earn a call, not twenty that pretend to replace one.</p>\n<p>For employed OB/GYN seats, clarify induction policies, backup for complex obstetrics, GYN OR access, and how APP collaboration actually works on a Tuesday afternoon. Then ask how compensation changes after any guarantee period. Public bands create permission to engage; private curves create permission to decide.</p>\n<h2>Use the physician funnel as a decision scaffold</h2>\n<p>AMP\u2019s physician path is Specialty \u2192 Region \u2192 Jobs list \u2192 Job View \u2192 Tap to Talk / Text / Email / chatbot \u2192 confirmation in The Mess Responses. That sequence is deliberate. It prevents the common failure mode where a physician bookmarks twelve PDFs and still cannot compare weeks. If you are comparing Midwest options, start with OBG-8449 as a calibration tease, then ask Amy Myers what else in the specialty corridor matches your non-negotiables.</p>\n<p>Bring your partner into the early filters: region, school constraints, visa or license timing, and tolerance for call. Summit meetings go better when the hike was honest from the trailhead.</p>\n",
"amp-score-explained": "\n<p>AMP Score is Adaptive Medical Partners\u2019 way of making \u201cfit\u201d less mystical. Physicians and organizations both drown in adjectives. AMP Score turns the mountain into measurable ridges: lifestyle, practice design, compensation clarity, culture signals, and readiness to meet at the summit.</p>\n<p>It is not a public ranking of hospitals. It is not a credit score for doctors. It is a guided explainer and intake lens that helps AMP route people faster \u2014 into the right specialty trail, the right conversation, and the right destination system (<strong>The Mess</strong> / <strong>BD Hub</strong>).</p>\n<h2>Why scoring belongs in recruiting</h2>\n<p>Without a scorecard, every opportunity feels like a vibe check. Committees disagree. Candidates ghost. Guides waste cycles. A simple scored model creates shared language: \u201cThis is a 78 on schedule integrity and a 62 on compensation clarity \u2014 worth a call, not a blind flyout.\u201d</p>\n<h2>The dimensions (interactive explainer)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Lifestyle fit</strong> \u2014 weekday endings, call load, commute, community pace.</li>\n<li><strong>Practice design</strong> \u2014 panel support, OR access, team model.</li>\n<li><strong>Compensation clarity</strong> \u2014 whether the story can be told cleanly on a short call.</li>\n<li><strong>Culture &amp; leadership</strong> \u2014 interview signal quality, not poster slogans.</li>\n<li><strong>Summit readiness</strong> \u2014 is the client prepared to meet a prepared candidate?</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>How physicians should use it</h2>\n<p>Treat AMP Score as a flashlight, not a verdict. Run the interactive experience on this site, then talk to a guide. If you are exploring OB/GYN in the Midwest, pair your score intuition with the OBG-8449 tease and a Tap to Talk \u2014 details stay gated on purpose.</p>\n<h2>How organizations should use it</h2>\n<p>If your role scores low on compensation clarity, fix the brief before you buy more sourcing. Retained search cannot market fog. AMP\u2019s client funnel shows a retained tease (upfront + placement + marketing) and then requests a meeting that lands in client intake and BD Hub \u2014 so business development and delivery share one story.</p>\n<div class=\"faq\">\n<details open><summary>Is AMP Score public for every job?</summary><p>No. Public pages stay tease-level. Scores inform guide conversations and guide conversations.</p></details>\n<details><summary>Does a high score mean I should say yes?</summary><p>No. It means the conversation is worth having. Decisions still belong to you and your family.</p></details>\n</div>\n<p><button class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-go=\"amp-score\">Try the AMP Score</button>\n<button class=\"btn btn-ghost\" data-go=\"education\">Back to Education hub</button></p>\n\n<h2>A note on Education and substance</h2>\n<p>Search engines reward helpful structure: clear H1/H2s, questions people actually ask, and helpful links to real next steps. This Education hub is packed on purpose \u2014 not as thin filler, but as trail markers toward the physician door, the client door, and sample opportunities like OBG-8449. Soft CTAs beat hard dossier dumps. When you are ready, Adaptive Medical Partners meets you with a guide, not a brochure cannon.</p>\n<p>Physicians comparing regions should also skim compensation clarity, schedule integrity, and summit readiness \u2014 the same themes inside AMP Score. Organizations should pair this reading with a retained process conversation so marketing, sourcing, and leadership share one brief. The mountain metaphor stays consistent: hikers on one side, clients at the summit, AMP guiding the traverse so the meeting is earned and prepared.</p>\n<p>If you take nothing else from this article, take the destination discipline. Every serious clickthrough on this site ends in The Mess and/or BD Hub. That is how a world-class recruiting site behaves when it is built for operations, not just aesthetics.</p>\n\n<h2>How the interactive score works on this site</h2>\n<p>The AMP Score page on this site is an explainer plus a simple interactive experience. You answer a few prompts about schedule sensitivity, compensation clarity needs, and culture priorities. The experience returns an illustrative score \u2014 for example a strong 78 \u2014 and invites a next step into the physician or client door. It is intentionally not a black-box AI verdict. It is a conversation starter aligned to AMP\u2019s guide model.</p>\n<p>In practice, the score helps AMP guide the conversation. A candidate chasing schedule integrity should not be delayed by a role that cannot state clinic end times. A client with fuzzy economics should get a briefing workshop before a national campaign. Score gaps become workplans.</p>\n<h2>Education, search visibility, and the score</h2>\n<p>AMP Score content belongs in the Education hub beside the blog because physicians and administrators both search for clearer ways to compare fit. Pair this article with retained-vs-contingent guidance and the sample job tease. Helpful links help readers and search engines understand that Adaptive Medical Partners is building a system \u2014 mountain story, funnels, capture, Mess/BD Hub destinations \u2014 not a static brochure site.</p>\n<p>When you finish the interactive experience, take one action: talk to a guide or request a meeting. Scores without destinations are parlor tricks. AMP refuses that dead end.</p>\n\n<h2>Keep the guide in the loop</h2>\n<p>Whether you are a physician evaluating a Midwest OB/GYN tease or an organization weighing retained support, Adaptive Medical Partners exists to keep the story coherent across marketing, recruiting, and leadership. Use Education pages to learn, doors to act, and Mess / BD Hub destinations to confirm that interest is operationally owned. That is the standard this mountain site is built to demonstrate \u2014 polished enough for AMP’s brand standard, disciplined enough for real inquiry handling, and never a full package dump in public view.</p>\n<p>From Irving, Texas, AMP\u2019s team helps candidates and clients meet at the summit prepared. Explore your route, pick a door, and let the trail transitions carry you through the same motion language whether you are hiking as a candidate or waiting as a client. The guide model only works if every path ends somewhere humans monitor \u2014 and on this site, that somewhere is named on purpose.</p>\n",
"midwest-lifestyle-for-physicians": "\n<p>\u201cMidwest lifestyle\u201d is either a clich\u00e9 or a competitive advantage, depending on whether anyone will define it. For physicians, lifestyle is not a lake photo. It is whether Monday\u2013Friday actually ends, whether schools and partners\u2019 careers work, and whether the clinic\u2019s culture matches the postcard.</p>\n<p>AMP leans into lifestyle heroes on job pages precisely because physicians decide with their whole life \u2014 then we refuse to pretend a photo replaces a package conversation. The Midwest OB/GYN sample <strong>OBG-8449</strong> leads with a weekday that ends at 4 and a public income band from $812k. That is lifestyle with a number attached, still gated where it should be.</p>\n<h2>What Midwest often gets right</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Community employed models with clearer panels than coastal auction markets.</li>\n<li>Shorter commutes and lower housing friction in many metros and mid-size cities.</li>\n<li>Space \u2014 literal and cultural \u2014 for a life outside clinic.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>What you must still pressure-test</h2>\n<p>Winter is real. Specialist density varies. \u201cFriendly\u201d can mean supportive or mean conflict-avoidant. Ask your guide for schedule integrity stories, not tourism boards. AMP\u2019s physician funnel \u2014 Specialty \u2192 Region \u2192 Jobs \u2192 View \u2014 is built so Midwest is a filter, not a fog machine.</p>\n<h2>Family and dual-career reality</h2>\n<p>Bring partner career constraints early. A summit meeting that ignores dual-career logistics is a delayed no. Guides who route interest into <strong>The Mess Responses</strong> can coordinate confidentially without broadcasting your search.</p>\n<div class=\"faq\">\n<details open><summary>Is OBG-8449 a real public tease?</summary><p>Yes \u2014 it is AMP\u2019s sample Midwest OB/GYN tease used in this site: practice-first headline, \u22645 bullets, contact to Amy Myers, no full dossier dump.</p></details>\n<details><summary>How do I start?</summary><p><button class=\"linkish\" data-go=\"physician\" data-trail=\"1\">Enter the physician trail</button> and choose OB/GYN \u2192 Midwest.</p></details>\n</div>\n\n<h2>A note on Education and substance</h2>\n<p>Search engines reward helpful structure: clear H1/H2s, questions people actually ask, and helpful links to real next steps. This Education hub is packed on purpose \u2014 not as thin filler, but as trail markers toward the physician door, the client door, and sample opportunities like OBG-8449. Soft CTAs beat hard dossier dumps. When you are ready, Adaptive Medical Partners meets you with a guide, not a brochure cannon.</p>\n<p>Physicians comparing regions should also skim compensation clarity, schedule integrity, and summit readiness \u2014 the same themes inside AMP Score. Organizations should pair this reading with a retained process conversation so marketing, sourcing, and leadership share one brief. The mountain metaphor stays consistent: hikers on one side, clients at the summit, AMP guiding the traverse so the meeting is earned and prepared.</p>\n<p>If you take nothing else from this article, take the destination discipline. Every serious clickthrough on this site ends in The Mess and/or BD Hub. That is how a world-class recruiting site behaves when it is built for operations, not just aesthetics.</p>\n\n<h2>Building a Midwest shortlist without self-sabotage</h2>\n<p>Create three buckets: must-have schedule truths, income floor for a serious conversation, and family constraints. Run AMP\u2019s region filter, then open teasers with lifestyle heroes and practice-first headlines. If a page leads with a hospital brand and hides the week, invert your attention \u2014 ask for the week first.</p>\n<p>Community size is not morality. Some physicians thrive in mid-size referral hubs; others want metro amenities with a community employed contract. Midwest contains both. What it often shares is a cultural willingness to talk plainly about call and panel \u2014 if you have a guide who knows how to ask.</p>\n<h2>From lifestyle photo to Mess Responses</h2>\n<p>When OBG-8449 (or another tease) hits your bar, do not stall in private browser tabs. Call, text, or email Amy Myers with CC to inquire@, or use the chatbot switchboard. Confirmation screens in this site show The Mess Responses so you can see the operational landing zone. Lifestyle decisions deserve operational seriousness.</p>\n<p>Also read AMP Score and the interview-prep-without-leaks guide before summit meetings. Arrive as a prepared hiker. That is how Midwest opportunities stop being postcards and start being choices.</p>\n\n<h2>Keep the guide in the loop</h2>\n<p>Whether you are a physician evaluating a Midwest OB/GYN tease or an organization weighing retained support, Adaptive Medical Partners exists to keep the story coherent across marketing, recruiting, and leadership. Use Education pages to learn, doors to act, and Mess / BD Hub destinations to confirm that interest is operationally owned. That is the standard this mountain site is built to demonstrate \u2014 polished enough for AMP’s brand standard, disciplined enough for real inquiry handling, and never a full package dump in public view.</p>\n<p>From Irving, Texas, AMP\u2019s team helps candidates and clients meet at the summit prepared. Explore your route, pick a door, and let the trail transitions carry you through the same motion language whether you are hiking as a candidate or waiting as a client. The guide model only works if every path ends somewhere humans monitor \u2014 and on this site, that somewhere is named on purpose.</p>\n\n<h2>Practical next step</h2>\n<p>If this article clarified your thinking, take one concrete step on this site within five minutes: open the physician door, open the client door, run AMP Score, or view the OBG-8449 tease. Education without action is just altitude sickness. Adaptive Medical Partners built the mountain clickthrough so learning and follow-up share the same trail system \u2014 with The Mess and BD Hub as named destinations and no public dossier leaks along the way.</p>\n",
"what-hospitals-should-ask-retained-firm": "\n<p>Before you retain a physician search firm, interview them like a strategic partner \u2014 because they will speak for your organization in the market. Soft questions get soft answers. Use the list below. AMP expects to answer all of them.</p>\n<h2>Twelve questions that separate guides from vendors</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Who owns the search day to day, and what else are they carrying?</li>\n<li>What does week one look like in writing?</li>\n<li>How do you market without leaking confidential details?</li>\n<li>What is your definition of a qualified slate \u2014 count or quality?</li>\n<li>How do candidates enter your response system (name the system)?</li>\n<li>How do client meetings get scheduled and briefed?</li>\n<li>What is included in upfront vs placement vs marketing?</li>\n<li>How do you handle competing specialties or shifting leadership?</li>\n<li>Show a redacted example of a job tease you are proud of.</li>\n<li>What happens when a search stalls at committee?</li>\n<li>How do you coordinate with in-house recruitment?</li>\n<li>Where does interest live after a form submit \u2014 inbox or operations hub?</li>\n</ol>\n<p>AMP\u2019s honest answers point to destinations: physician interest \u2192 <strong>The Mess Responses</strong>; client intake \u2192 <strong>The Mess</strong> + <strong>BD Hub</strong>. If a firm cannot name an operational destination, they are selling PDFs.</p>\n<h2>Retained tease vs contract dump</h2>\n<p>Your first meeting should clarify economics at a tease level: upfront commitment, placement alignment, marketing support. You should not need a 30-page MSA to understand the trail. AMP\u2019s client funnel is built that way on purpose.</p>\n<div class=\"faq\">\n<details open><summary>How do we start with AMP?</summary><p>Take the client door: Facility type \u2192 Specialty \u2192 Retained tease \u2192 Request meeting.</p></details>\n<details><summary>Do you work only retained?</summary><p>Ask on the meeting. This site emphasizes retained craft because confidential, strategic seats need guides.</p></details>\n</div>\n<p><button class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-go=\"client\" data-trail=\"1\">I\u2019m hiring \u2014 start client path</button></p>\n\n<h2>A note on Education and substance</h2>\n<p>Search engines reward helpful structure: clear H1/H2s, questions people actually ask, and helpful links to real next steps. This Education hub is packed on purpose \u2014 not as thin filler, but as trail markers toward the physician door, the client door, and sample opportunities like OBG-8449. Soft CTAs beat hard dossier dumps. When you are ready, Adaptive Medical Partners meets you with a guide, not a brochure cannon.</p>\n<p>Physicians comparing regions should also skim compensation clarity, schedule integrity, and summit readiness \u2014 the same themes inside AMP Score. Organizations should pair this reading with a retained process conversation so marketing, sourcing, and leadership share one brief. The mountain metaphor stays consistent: hikers on one side, clients at the summit, AMP guiding the traverse so the meeting is earned and prepared.</p>\n<p>If you take nothing else from this article, take the destination discipline. Every serious clickthrough on this site ends in The Mess and/or BD Hub. That is how a world-class recruiting site behaves when it is built for operations, not just aesthetics.</p>\n\n<h2>Align leadership before you retain anyone</h2>\n<p>The fastest way to burn retained fees is an unclear brief. Chief medical officers, service line leaders, and finance must agree on schedule, income story, and what \u201cgood\u201d looks like in a slate. AMP\u2019s client path \u2014 Facility type \u2192 Specialty \u2192 Retained tease \u2192 Request meeting \u2014 is designed to surface misalignment early, while the conversation is still a meeting request rather than a stalled national campaign.</p>\n<p>Ask how the firm will brief candidates for summit meetings without oversharing. No-leaks discipline protects your brand in the physician community. Physicians talk. Overexposure of unfinished economics teaches the market that your process is unfinished.</p>\n<h2>BD Hub and delivery should share a spine</h2>\n<p>Business development that sells one story while delivery runs another creates distrust. AMP\u2019s confirmation UI shows client intake in The Mess and a BD Hub path on purpose. When you evaluate firms, ask to see the handoff. If marketing, sourcing, and sales and delivery teams cannot point to the same destination system, you will feel that fracture in week four.</p>\n<p>Use the Education hub, this checklist, and a request-meeting form to start cleanly. Retained search is a guided climb. Inspect the guide\u2019s kit before you rope in.</p>\n\n<h2>Keep the guide in the loop</h2>\n<p>Whether you are a physician evaluating a Midwest OB/GYN tease or an organization weighing retained support, Adaptive Medical Partners exists to keep the story coherent across marketing, recruiting, and leadership. Use Education pages to learn, doors to act, and Mess / BD Hub destinations to confirm that interest is operationally owned. That is the standard this mountain site is built to demonstrate \u2014 polished enough for AMP’s brand standard, disciplined enough for real inquiry handling, and never a full package dump in public view.</p>\n<p>From Irving, Texas, AMP\u2019s team helps candidates and clients meet at the summit prepared. Explore your route, pick a door, and let the trail transitions carry you through the same motion language whether you are hiking as a candidate or waiting as a client. The guide model only works if every path ends somewhere humans monitor \u2014 and on this site, that somewhere is named on purpose.</p>\n",
"candidate-interview-prep-without-leaks": "\n<p>Interview prep fails in two opposite ways: candidates walk in cold, or someone forwards them an internal packet that was never meant to leave the building. AMP\u2019s no-leaks standard exists so you can be sharp without turning confidential client material into public lore.</p>\n<h2>What good prep includes</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Your story in 90 seconds: training, what you want in a week, what you will not trade away.</li>\n<li>Practice-first questions (schedule, call, team) ahead of city trivia.</li>\n<li>Compensation questions that invite structure, not poker tells.</li>\n<li>Clarity on timeline and decision partners at home.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>What stays gated</h2>\n<p>Named facility deep-dives, non-public financials, and anything marked confidential stay on the call with your guide and the summit meeting \u2014 not in group chats, not on public job pages. Public teasers like OBG-8449 exist to start the hike, not to finish the map.</p>\n<h2>How AMP routes you</h2>\n<p>Tap to Talk / Text / Email / chatbot all converge on <strong>The Mess Responses</strong> for the owner recruiter. That is how prep stays coordinated: one thread, one guide, one mountain story.</p>\n<div class=\"faq\">\n<details open><summary>Can I see the full package before a call?</summary><p>You will see a controlled tease publicly. Full stacks are shared in confidential conversation \u2014 by design.</p></details>\n<details><summary>Who do I contact for OBG-8449?</summary><p>Amy Myers \u00b7 972-301-2261 \u00b7 amyers@adaptivemedicalpartners.com \u00b7 CC inquire@adaptivemedicalpartners.com</p></details>\n</div>\n<p><button class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-go=\"job/obg-8449\">View OBG-8449</button>\n<button class=\"btn btn-ghost\" data-go=\"chat\" data-trail=\"1\">Open chatbot switchboard</button></p>\n\n<h2>A note on Education and substance</h2>\n<p>Search engines reward helpful structure: clear H1/H2s, questions people actually ask, and helpful links to real next steps. This Education hub is packed on purpose \u2014 not as thin filler, but as trail markers toward the physician door, the client door, and sample opportunities like OBG-8449. Soft CTAs beat hard dossier dumps. When you are ready, Adaptive Medical Partners meets you with a guide, not a brochure cannon.</p>\n<p>Physicians comparing regions should also skim compensation clarity, schedule integrity, and summit readiness \u2014 the same themes inside AMP Score. Organizations should pair this reading with a retained process conversation so marketing, sourcing, and leadership share one brief. The mountain metaphor stays consistent: hikers on one side, clients at the summit, AMP guiding the traverse so the meeting is earned and prepared.</p>\n<p>If you take nothing else from this article, take the destination discipline. Every serious clickthrough on this site ends in The Mess and/or BD Hub. That is how a world-class recruiting site behaves when it is built for operations, not just aesthetics.</p>\n\n<h2>Practice questions that sound senior</h2>\n<p>Instead of \u201cWhat\u2019s the comp?\u201d try \u201cWalk me through how a strong first-year physician succeeds economically here after any guarantee.\u201d Instead of \u201cIs the call bad?\u201d try \u201cWhen clinic runs long, who absorbs labor coverage, and how often does that happen in a typical month?\u201d Senior questions invite structure. Structure is what confidential conversations are for.</p>\n<p>Rehearse your non-negotiables aloud with your guide. AMP can pressure-test them against the tease you saw publicly without emailing you a forbidden packet. That is the point of controlled reveal.</p>\n<h2>After the interview</h2>\n<p>Send a concise thank-you through your recruiter of record. Do not freelance confidential details into thank-you notes. Log questions that remain open and ask AMP to close them before you invest in a second visit. Interest and follow-ups still route through The Mess Responses so the owner recruiter keeps the thread coherent.</p>\n<p>If you are early and only browsing, stay in Education and Search until a tease earns a Tap to Talk. The mountain is climbed in stages. Skipping stages is how people get lost \u2014 or how leaks start.</p>\n\n<h2>Keep the guide in the loop</h2>\n<p>Whether you are a physician evaluating a Midwest OB/GYN tease or an organization weighing retained support, Adaptive Medical Partners exists to keep the story coherent across marketing, recruiting, and leadership. Use Education pages to learn, doors to act, and Mess / BD Hub destinations to confirm that interest is operationally owned. That is the standard this mountain site is built to demonstrate \u2014 polished enough for AMP’s brand standard, disciplined enough for real inquiry handling, and never a full package dump in public view.</p>\n<p>From Irving, Texas, AMP\u2019s team helps candidates and clients meet at the summit prepared. Explore your route, pick a door, and let the trail transitions carry you through the same motion language whether you are hiking as a candidate or waiting as a client. The guide model only works if every path ends somewhere humans monitor \u2014 and on this site, that somewhere is named on purpose.</p>\n\n<h2>Practical next step</h2>\n<p>If this article clarified your thinking, take one concrete step on this site within five minutes: open the physician door, open the client door, run AMP Score, or view the OBG-8449 tease. Education without action is just altitude sickness. Adaptive Medical Partners built the mountain clickthrough so learning and follow-up share the same trail system \u2014 with The Mess and BD Hub as named destinations and no public dossier leaks along the way.</p>\n",
"physician-retention-3-year-kpi": "\n<p>Most physician recruiting dashboards still worship a single idol: time-to-fill. Fill the seat. Close the req. Move on. That metric is not wrong \u2014 vacancies hurt patients and burn out the physicians who remain \u2014 but it is incomplete. The mountain question is different: <strong>did they stay?</strong></p>\n<p>Adaptive Medical Partners publishes a public proof point from our About language: <strong>87% retention at three years</strong>. That is not a brochure adjective. It is the KPI that tells a hospital whether the summit meeting produced a lasting partner or a expensive revolving door. In mountain terms: placement is reaching the peak; retention is staying on the mountain.</p>\n<h2>Why \u201cfilled\u201d is a soft finish line</h2>\n<p>A filled role can hide a failed match. The physician who leaves at month fourteen takes institutional memory, referral relationships, and team trust with them. Committees restart. Locums spend spikes. Candidates in the market hear that your organization churns. Contingent firms that optimize for speed-to-resume rarely own that second-order damage. Retained guides should \u2014 because their economics and craft are supposed to fund fit, not just volume.</p>\n<p>AMP\u2019s story is explicit. Candidates are <strong>searching for the peak</strong>. Clients are <strong>waiting at the peak</strong> while AMP does the climb work. If the wait was for the wrong hiker, the work was wasted. Retention is how you know the wait was worth it.</p>\n<h2>Three-year retention as an operating system</h2>\n<p>Three years is long enough to clear the honeymoon and short enough to be honest. It covers schedule reality versus brochure promises, income curve versus guarantee theater, culture versus posters, and family logistics versus city nicknames. When AMP says 87% are still there at three years, we are saying the guided climb produced durable fit \u2014 not that every search was easy.</p>\n<p>Organizations evaluating firms should ask:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>How do you define retention (employed physician still in role / system)?</li>\n<li>What inputs do you pressure-test before a summit meeting?</li>\n<li>How does marketing stay tease-level so the market does not decide for you?</li>\n<li>Where do responses live after interest \u2014 a named destination or a vanishing inbox?</li>\n</ul>\n<p>AMP\u2019s honest answers point to destinations: physician interest \u2192 <strong>The Mess Responses</strong>; client meetings \u2192 <strong>The Mess</strong> + <strong>BD Hub</strong>. Retention craft without operational ownership is a slide, not a system.</p>\n<h2>Retention, interview efficiency, and rural reality</h2>\n<p>Retention does not live alone. AMP also cites <strong>1.7 average interviews per successful placement</strong> and partnerships with <strong>700+ rural hospitals / FQHCs / critical access facilities</strong>, with <strong>16 years</strong> of tenure since 2010. Together those numbers describe a guide model: prepare hikers, do not waste summit meetings, know community-fit corridors, and stay long enough to learn what lasts.</p>\n<p>Read the companion pillars on <a href=\"#blog/interview-to-placement-efficiency\" data-go=\"blog/interview-to-placement-efficiency\">interview-to-placement efficiency</a> and <a href=\"#blog/rural-fqhc-critical-access-recruiting\" data-go=\"blog/rural-fqhc-critical-access-recruiting\">rural / FQHC recruiting</a>. Retention is the north face; those articles are the ridges that support it.</p>\n<h2>What hospitals should change this quarter</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Score stay risk in the brief</strong> \u2014 schedule integrity, call architecture, dual-career constraints, leadership responsiveness.</li>\n<li><strong>Refuse dossier dumps in public</strong> \u2014 tease enough to attract the right searchers; gate economics on a call.</li>\n<li><strong>Demand slate quality over resume count</strong> \u2014 summit-ready beats \u201cwe sent forty.\u201d</li>\n<li><strong>Align BD and delivery</strong> \u2014 if sales promises one mountain and recruiting delivers another, retention collapses.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Physician lens: why stay rates protect you too</h2>\n<p>If you are searching for the peak, a firm obsessed only with fill speed may rush you into a Tuesday that never ends. Ask how they talk about three-year outcomes. Ask for a controlled tease like Midwest OB/GYN <strong>OBG-8449</strong> \u2014 practice-first, \u22645 bullets, package on a short call \u2014 then Tap to Talk. Soft CTAs beat hard pressure.</p>\n<div class=\"faq\">\n<details open><summary>Is 87% retention a guarantee for my search?</summary><p>No public stat is a personal guarantee. It is AMP\u2019s reported three-year retention proof from About language \u2014 evidence of craft, not a contract clause on this page.</p></details>\n<details><summary>How is this different from time-to-fill marketing?</summary><p>Time-to-fill asks how fast the seat closed. Retention asks whether the close was worth it three years later. Serious organizations track both.</p></details>\n<details><summary>Where should hiring leaders go next?</summary><p>Take the client door for a retained tease and meeting request into The Mess + BD Hub, or read <a href=\"#blog/retained-vs-contingent-physician-search\" data-go=\"blog/retained-vs-contingent-physician-search\">retained vs contingent</a>.</p></details>\n<details><summary>What about candidates comparing OB/GYN roles?</summary><p>Start at the physician door or open the <button class=\"linkish\" data-go=\"job/obg-8449\">OBG-8449 tease</button> \u2014 then talk to Amy Myers. Full stacks stay gated on purpose.</p></details>\n</div>\n<p><button class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-go=\"client\" data-trail=\"1\">I\u2019m hiring \u2014 hold the peak</button>\n<button class=\"btn btn-ghost\" data-go=\"physician\" data-trail=\"1\">I\u2019m a physician \u2014 search for the peak</button>\n<button class=\"btn btn-ghost\" data-go=\"job/obg-8449\">Sample job OBG-8449</button></p>\n<h2>Mountain metaphor without the fog</h2>\n<p>Guides do not measure success only by whether someone touched the summit cairn. They measure whether the climber can live at elevation. AMP\u2019s 87% at three years is that idea in a number. Pair it with 1.7 interview efficiency and 700+ community partnerships, and you get a recruiting narrative that can win search and AI overviews without inventing towns or leaking dossiers.</p>\n<p>Education without a destination is altitude sickness. When this article clarifies your thinking, take one step: request a meeting, open a physician trail, or run AMP Score. Interest should land where humans monitor it \u2014 on this site, that is The Mess and BD Hub.</p>\n",
"interview-to-placement-efficiency": "\n<p>\u201cWe interviewed twelve people\u201d is not a flex if eleven were never summit-ready. Adaptive Medical Partners cites a sharper efficiency story from our About language: <strong>1.7 average interviews per successful placement</strong>. In mountain terms, that means prepared hikers arrive at the peak \u2014 AMP does not waste the client\u2019s summit meetings on tourists who were never going to rope in.</p>\n<p>Interview-to-placement efficiency is the twin of retention. AMP also reports <strong>87% retention at three years</strong>, partnerships with <strong>700+ rural hospitals / FQHCs / critical access facilities</strong>, and <strong>16 years</strong> since 2010. Efficiency without stay rates is a clever funnel; together they describe a guide model.</p>\n<h2>What 1.7 actually buys a hospital</h2>\n<p>Every interview consumes CMO time, panel coordination, travel or virtual theater, and emotional bandwidth for the service line. Inflated interview counts often signal fuzzy briefs, spray-and-pray sourcing, or contingent races where five firms send five versions of the same seat. A 1.7 average says the hard work happened on the trail: education, filtering, and staging \u2014 before anyone sits down at the summit.</p>\n<p>Clients on AMP\u2019s path are <strong>waiting at the peak</strong> while AMP does the climb work. Candidates are <strong>searching for the peak</strong>. The guide\u2019s job is to make the wait short in wasted meetings, not short in diligence.</p>\n<h2>Efficiency is not ruthlessness</h2>\n<p>Low interview counts can be a red flag if they mean the firm hid options or pressured a yes. Healthy efficiency looks like:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A written week-one workplan and specialty scorecard</li>\n<li>Tease-level public marketing that attracts the right searchers</li>\n<li>Confidential package conversations before flyouts</li>\n<li>Interview prep without leaks</li>\n<li>Responses landing in a named system humans monitor</li>\n</ul>\n<p>AMP\u2019s physician funnel \u2014 Specialty \u2192 Region \u2192 Jobs \u2192 Tease \u2192 Tap to Talk / Text / Email / Chat \u2192 <strong>The Mess Responses</strong> \u2014 is built to educate before the summit. The client funnel \u2014 Facility \u2192 Specialty \u2192 Retained tease \u2192 Request meeting \u2192 <strong>The Mess</strong> + <strong>BD Hub</strong> \u2014 is built so leadership waits with a brief worth defending.</p>\n<h2>How retained craft funds fewer, better meetings</h2>\n<p>Retained economics pay for outbound that is not optional, specialty expertise, and marketing craft. Contingent economics often pay for a race. Read <a href=\"#blog/retained-vs-contingent-physician-search\" data-go=\"blog/retained-vs-contingent-physician-search\">retained vs contingent</a> for the model comparison. The 1.7 story is what retained should feel like operationally: not endless first dates.</p>\n<p>Pacific-style closing-ratio boasts are useful market language; AMP\u2019s interview efficiency is more precise for committee calendars. Do not invent competitor numbers \u2014 stick to AMP\u2019s About stats and ask every firm for their definition of \u201cinterview\u201d and \u201csuccessful placement.\u201d</p>\n<h2>Candidate experience is part of efficiency</h2>\n<p>Physicians ghost processes that waste their evenings. A guided tease like <strong>OBG-8449</strong> (Monday\u2013Friday that ends at 4, from $812k public band, Midwest employed) lets a searcher decide whether 90 seconds of interest is warranted \u2014 without a dossier dump. Prep stays ethical: practice questions, non-negotiables, timeline clarity \u2014 not confidential packets. See <a href=\"#blog/candidate-interview-prep-without-leaks\" data-go=\"blog/candidate-interview-prep-without-leaks\">interview prep without leaks</a>.</p>\n<h2>Questions to ask any firm about interview math</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>What counts as an interview in your 1.x metric?</li>\n<li>How do you prevent duplicate stories from multiple recruiters?</li>\n<li>What disqualifies a candidate before a summit meeting?</li>\n<li>Where do candidate and client responses live after the form?</li>\n<li>How do you connect interview efficiency to three-year retention?</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"faq\">\n<details open><summary>Does 1.7 mean AMP only presents two candidates ever?</summary><p>It means average interviews per successful placement \u2014 a process efficiency claim from About language \u2014 not a promise that every search has a two-person slate.</p></details>\n<details><summary>How does this relate to 87% retention?</summary><p>Prepared meetings should produce better matches. Retention is the three-year check that efficiency was not just speed theater. Read the <a href=\"#blog/physician-retention-3-year-kpi\" data-go=\"blog/physician-retention-3-year-kpi\">retention pillar</a>.</p></details>\n<details><summary>I\u2019m hiring \u2014 what\u2019s the soft next step?</summary><p><button class=\"linkish\" data-go=\"client\" data-trail=\"1\">Hold the peak \u2014 client path</button> for facility \u2192 specialty \u2192 retained tease \u2192 meeting request into The Mess + BD Hub.</p></details>\n<details><summary>I\u2019m a physician searching \u2014 where do I start?</summary><p><button class=\"linkish\" data-go=\"physician\" data-trail=\"1\">Enter the physician search</button> or view <button class=\"linkish\" data-go=\"job/obg-8449\">OBG-8449</button>.</p></details>\n</div>\n<p><button class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-go=\"client\" data-trail=\"1\">Request a retained conversation</button>\n<button class=\"btn btn-ghost\" data-go=\"job/obg-8449\">Open OBG-8449 tease</button>\n<button class=\"btn btn-ghost\" data-go=\"education\">Education hub</button></p>\n<h2>Search visibility, AI answers, and the efficiency story</h2>\n<p>Search and AI overviews cite consistent entities. Repeating <strong>1.7 / 87% / 700+ / 16 years</strong> with source discipline (AMP About; no invented extras like day-count guarantees until confirmed) helps AMP own the interview-efficiency cluster without sounding like every other staffing blog. FAQ blocks, internal links to doors, and a sample job tease complete the funnel from TOFU education to BOFU capture.</p>\n<p>When you finish this article, take one action in under five minutes. Efficiency articles that end in nowhere teach the wrong lesson. AMP\u2019s mountain site experience ends in The Mess and BD Hub on purpose. Committees should also compare how firms brief both sides before the first summit meeting: shared scorecards, non-negotiables, and a single owner recruiter reduce duplicate interviews that inflate counts without improving outcomes. That discipline is how 1.7 stays a feature of craft \u2014 not a slogan \u2014 while candidates keep searching for the peak and clients keep waiting at the peak while AMP does the work.</p>\n",
"rural-fqhc-critical-access-recruiting": "\n<p>Community hospitals, FQHCs, and critical access facilities do not lose physicians for mysterious reasons. They lose them when the week was mis-sold, the call pool was fiction, the town was a postcard instead of a plan, or the firm treated rural as a discount channel. Adaptive Medical Partners\u2019 About language includes a scale proof that matters here: partnerships with <strong>700+ rural hospitals / FQHCs / critical access facilities</strong>, alongside <strong>87% retention at three years</strong>, <strong>1.7 average interviews per successful placement</strong>, and <strong>16 years</strong> since 2010.</p>\n<p>This pillar is about that corridor \u2014 without inventing hospital names or towns. Region-level lifestyle and practice-first teasers beat fake local search visibility. Candidates are <strong>searching for the peak</strong>; clients are <strong>waiting at the peak</strong> while AMP does the work. Rural peaks are real peaks. They deserve guides, not spray.</p>\n<h2>What \u201crural / FQHC / CAH\u201d actually demands</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Schedule honesty</strong> \u2014 weekday endings, backup, APP collaboration, obstetrics coverage where relevant.</li>\n<li><strong>Mission without martyrdom</strong> \u2014 FQHC purpose is a magnet; burnout theater is not.</li>\n<li><strong>Family logistics</strong> \u2014 partner careers, schools, housing, distance to tertiary care.</li>\n<li><strong>Income clarity on a call</strong> \u2014 public bands where appropriate; curves gated.</li>\n<li><strong>Confidentiality</strong> \u2014 many community searches cannot survive a dossier leak.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>AMP\u2019s retained tease \u2014 upfront + placement + marketing \u2014 exists so organizations can fund real outbound and tease-level creative without dumping full economics onto the open web. Meeting requests land in <strong>The Mess</strong> with a <strong>BD Hub</strong> path so business development and delivery share one spine.</p>\n<h2>Why 700+ partnerships are an search visibility and trust signal</h2>\n<p>Authority in healthcare recruiting is earned through repetition of true scale claims plus useful methodology content. \u201c700+\u201d tells searchers AMP has pattern recognition across community settings \u2014 not that every facility should be named on a blog. Pair the number with retention and interview efficiency so AI systems cite a coherent organization, not a single vanity metric.</p>\n<p>Competitors publish city lifestyle posts and placement maps. Steal the structure; keep AMP\u2019s guardrails: no invented facilities, controlled reveals, named named destinations, mountain story that distinguishes candidate search from client wait at the peak.</p>\n<h2>Practical recruiting playbook for community seats</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Write the Tuesday before you buy a national campaign.</li>\n<li>Decide what is public tease versus call-gated (see sample <strong>OBG-8449</strong> for practice-first discipline).</li>\n<li>Brief leadership on summit readiness \u2014 who interviews, how fast, what \u201cyes\u201d requires.</li>\n<li>Route every physician tap into The Mess Responses with an owner recruiter.</li>\n<li>Measure stay risk alongside time-to-slate quality.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Candidate guidance without romanticizing \u201crural\u201d</h2>\n<p>Midwest and other regions contain mid-size hubs and true critical access settings. Neither is morally superior. Pressure-test commute, call, panel support, and dual-career reality. Use Education posts like <a href=\"#blog/midwest-lifestyle-for-physicians\" data-go=\"blog/midwest-lifestyle-for-physicians\">Midwest lifestyle</a> and <a href=\"#blog/evaluate-obgyn-opportunities\" data-go=\"blog/evaluate-obgyn-opportunities\">evaluating OB/GYN opportunities</a>, then enter the physician door. Soft next step: talk to a guide before you fall in love with a lake photo.</p>\n<h2>FQHC and critical access specifics</h2>\n<p>FQHCs need recruiters who understand sliding-fee culture, grant rhythms, and provider wellness \u2014 not only RVU mythology. Critical access facilities need honesty about transfer patterns, ED coverage expectations, and community visibility (you will be known at the grocery store). AMP\u2019s guide model is built for those conversations; contingent blasts often are not.</p>\n<div class=\"faq\">\n<details open><summary>Will AMP list my hospital on this page?</summary><p>No. Public education stays facility-agnostic on purpose. Partnerships are cited at the 700+ aggregate level from About language.</p></details>\n<details><summary>How do retention and rural fit connect?</summary><p>Community roles punish mismatch faster. 87% three-year retention is the stay-rate proof; rural craft is where that proof is stress-tested. Read the <a href=\"#blog/physician-retention-3-year-kpi\" data-go=\"blog/physician-retention-3-year-kpi\">retention pillar</a>.</p></details>\n<details><summary>What\u2019s the client next step?</summary><p><button class=\"linkish\" data-go=\"client\" data-trail=\"1\">Hold the peak — client path</button> \u2014 facility type includes FQHC / community health \u2014 then retained tease and meeting request.</p></details>\n<details><summary>What\u2019s the physician next step?</summary><p><button class=\"linkish\" data-go=\"physician\" data-trail=\"1\">Search for the peak</button> or open <button class=\"linkish\" data-go=\"job/obg-8449\">OBG-8449</button> as a calibration tease.</p></details>\n</div>\n<p><button class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-go=\"client\" data-trail=\"1\">I\u2019m hiring for a community / FQHC seat</button>\n<button class=\"btn btn-ghost\" data-go=\"physician\" data-trail=\"1\">I\u2019m a physician exploring community roles</button>\n<button class=\"btn btn-ghost\" data-go=\"blog/interview-to-placement-efficiency\">Read the 1.7 efficiency pillar</button></p>\n<h2>Close the loop to Mess / BD Hub</h2>\n<p>World-class rural recruiting content fails if it ends in a PDF. AMP\u2019s mountain clickthrough keeps Education \u2192 doors \u2192 tease \u2192 Tap to Talk / Request meeting \u2192 named destinations. Sixteen years since 2010 is E-E-A-T; 700+ is scale; 1.7 and 87% are operating proof. Use them together. Omit unconfirmed day-count guarantees until confirmed for public use.</p>\n<p>If this guide helped, act inside five minutes. Community patients do not benefit from bookmarked articles alone \u2014 they benefit when a prepared candidate meets a prepared client at the peak. Keep linking Education to doors: retained vs contingent for model choice, retention for the stay-rate KPI, and interview efficiency for summit-meeting hygiene. Rural and FQHC leaders who want a meeting should say facility type plainly on the client path; physicians who want community pace should filter specialty and region, open a tease like OBG-8449 when it fits, and Tap to Talk so The Mess Responses owns the thread.</p>\n"
,
"first-job-shortfalls-residents-fellows": `<p>Coming out of training feels like reaching a ridge. The first offer can look like the summit because the fatigue is real and the next chapter is waiting. AMP’s job is not to push you toward a role; it is to help you see the trail conditions before you commit.</p><p>As reported by the AMA, nearly 60% of physicians leave their first post-training job within three years, and more than 25% consider leaving within the first year. Those numbers are a prompt for better questions, not a forecast for your future.</p><h2>Shortfall one: the week is a slogan</h2><p>Ask someone to walk you through their typical week. When does clinic end? Who covers when the schedule runs long? How often is call truly shared? “Collegial” is not a schedule. A clear week is a place to start.</p><h2>Shortfall two: place is treated as scenery</h2><p>Place is not a hero image. It is partner work, school timing, family distance, community fit, and the version of yourself you can sustain there. Read our <a href="#blog/place-first-without-regret" data-go="blog/place-first-without-regret">place-first guide</a> before you rank a region.</p><h2>Shortfall three: contract literacy arrives late</h2><p>The AMA points physicians toward retention questions, practice longevity and affiliations, and contract literacy. Ask what changes after any guarantee, how production is measured, what termination means, and who will explain the language without rushing you.</p><h2>Shortfall four: no second-climb plan</h2><p>A first job is important, not irreversible. Put a two-year audit on your calendar — don’t wait for year three, when too many first jobs have already gone sideways: week, support, leadership, economics, and your own energy. AMP’s three-year retention story is the goal; the two-year check is how you protect it. If the fit is wrong, a thoughtful second climb beats a quiet third year of regret.</p><p>AMP is the guide, not the headline. Use the physician path when you are ready, or send a short note to a named guide. Full packages belong in a conversation; your questions belong at the trailhead.</p>`,
"place-first-without-regret": `<p>Specialty is the trailhead. Place is the weather, pace, people, and life around the practice. Residents and fellows often start with a job title and only later ask whether the place can hold the life they want. AMP helps reverse that order without pretending a region has one story.</p><h2>Define place in human terms</h2><p>Write down what your household needs: partner work, school rhythm, family distance, airport access, outdoors, culture, and the kind of community where you want to be known. None of those answers is universal, and none needs a made-up town story.</p><h2>Then test the practice</h2><p>Place-first does not mean practice-last. Ask how the week fits the life you just described. A beautiful corridor cannot rescue a call schedule that breaks the promises you made at home. A strong team cannot erase a place your family cannot inhabit.</p><h2>Use regions as questions, not verdicts</h2><p>AMP’s region map is a starting point. Select more than one zone, compare the language, and ask a guide what is known versus what still needs a call. MGMA can provide compensation and practice context; associations and public benchmarks are useful context, not a personal offer.</p><h2>Make the summit conversation concrete</h2><ul><li>What does an ordinary Tuesday look like?</li><li>What support exists when volume or call spikes?</li><li>What affiliations and referral relationships shape the practice?</li><li>What would make you still want to be there in two years?</li></ul><p>Place-first without regret is not about finding a perfect postcard. It is about choosing a ridge whose trade-offs you can name. When you want help, AMP can guide the next question—and keep the full map off the public page.</p>`,
"two-year-test-before-you-sign": `<p>A contract can be accurate and still fail to answer the question that matters: can I live this job for two years? Before you sign, AMP recommends a simple audit. It is an education tool, not legal advice; have counsel review the document.</p><h2>Test the week</h2><p>Compare written hours, call, clinic volume, inpatient obligations, and coverage to the week described in interviews. Ask what happens during holidays, vacancies, and growth. If the schedule depends on everyone being fully staffed forever, you have found a risk worth naming.</p><h2>Test the economics</h2><p>Understand guarantee length, wRVU or other production mechanics, conversion points, bonuses, repayment language, and expenses. MGMA compensation benchmarks can be context for a conversation; they are not a promise for your specialty or place.</p><h2>Test the relationship</h2><p>Who answers when a contract question appears? Ask how leadership handles feedback, how physicians learn about change, and whether the team can describe practice longevity and affiliations. The AMA encourages physicians to ask retention questions when evaluating first jobs.</p><h2>Test the exit</h2><p>Read termination, notice, restrictive covenant, malpractice tail, and repayment sections slowly. A guide can help you build questions; counsel should advise you on rights and obligations.</p><h2>Keep your second climb visible</h2><p>Put a two-year check-in on your calendar before the first day. Score the week, support, contract reality, place, and energy. You are not planning to leave; you are keeping agency. If the first ridge needs a second climb, you will know why.</p><p>Ready to compare a role? AMP’s public pages stay tease-level. Tap to Talk when a practice earns the next conversation.</p>`,
"mpc-when-location-leads": `<p>Sometimes location leads. A resident has a partner constraint, a family corridor, or a clear picture of the place they can call home before the practice is known. That is a real filter. It is also where the MPC contrast can be useful.</p><h2>Wilderness + telescope</h2><p>AMP uses MPC as a wilderness-and-telescope contrast: a candidate explores an unknown peak without a retained seat or guide, while a contingent client looks from the peak with a telescope. It is a different path, not a promise of live inventory.</p><h2>When the location filter helps</h2><p>Use place as a first cut when it protects a real household need. Then ask whether the practice can support the life you are protecting. A location can lead the conversation without becoming the entire decision.</p><h2>When a guide adds value</h2><p>Retained search is the guided path: you are searching for the peak, the client is waiting at the peak, and AMP does the climb work between them. A guide can translate vague place language into questions about schedule, affiliations, support, and longevity.</p><p>There is no need to expose client names, search IDs, or a full MI file to learn whether a path deserves attention. Start with a ridge snapshot, then contact a guide if the example raises a real question. Tease → contact is the right sequence.</p>`,
"coordinator-share-pack-grads": `<p>Program coordinators can give graduating residents and fellows a short pack before the first job search. The goal is not to endorse a recruiter or a town. It is to help a new physician ask questions early.</p><h2>Share these five prompts</h2><ol><li>What does the ordinary week look like?</li><li>How does call work when the team is short?</li><li>What changes after the guarantee or first contract period?</li><li>How does the organization support practice longevity?</li><li>What would make this place sustainable for two years?</li></ol><h2>Point them to neutral context</h2><p>AMA reporting on early-career retention and contract literacy is a useful starting point. AAPPR 2025 benchmarking and MGMA association resources can add industry context; neither can choose a first job for an individual graduate.</p><h2>Keep the handoff human</h2><p>Encourage graduates to compare specialty, region, place, week, and contract. If they want help, a short conversation with a guide is more useful than a full public dossier. AMP’s residents hub offers rank, region, and an illustrative MI Lite peek before the next step.</p><p>Graduation is a summit, not the end of learning. Give each new physician a map, a checklist, and permission to ask one more question.</p>`
};
